  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1049 AM MDT Sun May 10 2026\n\nToday...we continue to watch high pressure strengthen just to our\nwest, as it slowly begins to inch east toward the Borderland. At \nthe lower levels we are in the midst of a fropa, with a frontal \npush working in across the CWA, from NE to SW, in a backdoor \nfashion. This feature is causing gusty NE winds today, which will \ntransition to breezy and gusty E winds overnight tonight, as the \nfront gets a nighttime push from the plains to our east. For this\nafternoon, the slight influx of moisture with this front will \nintroduce enough moisture over our eastern areas to see some \nconvective cloud development, with isolated to scattered shower \nand thunderstorm potential across the SACs as well as some of the \nsurrounding lower areas across eastern Sierra, and Dona Ana, as \nwell as Otero, and possibly N Hudspeth counties. Western and most \nCentral areas are expected to remain dry and mostly sunny. \n\nThis evening and overnight...the frontal boundary will get a \ntypical nighttime nudge, from surface high pressure building over \nthe plains, and push well west to the AZ border. It will do that \nwith some breezy and gusty easterly winds overnight. Current model\nguidance dose some some winds very near AWW criteria overnight, \nbut it does fall just short. Recent events have also been close, \nbut guidance for those episodes dropped off winds, and that \nverified, so we will not issue an AWW unless it become more \ncertain.\n\nTomorrow...through at least Tuesday looks warm and dry area wide \nas the upper ridge moves our way, and sit over the region. This \nwill allow for heat to build, and keep moisture out of the region.\nIt will also help to keep wind speeds down.\n\nWednesday/Thursday...the ridge axis begins to slip to the east, \nwhich allows a SW fetch on the backside, which will be on a \ntrajectory to come over our western zones. Both the GFS and the EC\nmodels suggest a bit of moisture in that flow, that results in a \nhint of showers or storms. Not yet in the NBM, so it doesn't show \nup in the forecast, but this serves as a heads up that, this \noccurrence could be in our future. \n\nThursday/Friday...could turn a bit breezy as the ridge exits and \nthe next Pacific low pressure system begins it's eastward track \ninto the southern Great Basin. That will shove the minor plume of \nmoisture to the east, and pull in some drier air on breezy W and \nSW winds both afternoons, it appears. \n\nSaturday/Sunday...the end of week trough passes dryly, well to our\nnorth, and we see a minor ridge reform over the Southwest Deserts.\nWe should continue with warmth, dry conditions, and lighter winds.",
